import{_ as r,o as i,c as a,a3 as t}from"./chunks/framework.iKZ1V-y0.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"2.4 “Faster-Than-Light” Upstream Propagation of Blocking Signals","description":"","frontmatter":{"lang":"en-US","translationSource":{"title":"2\\\\.4 阻塞信号の“超光速”反向传播规律","archivedAt":"2026-10-23T14:20:34.857Z","url":"https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=6aq5YsEznFHSQK7mwkc1Gm","scope":"page"}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"logistics/src/page-6aq5YsEznFHSQK7mwkc1Gm.md","filePath":"logistics/src/page-6aq5YsEznFHSQK7mwkc1Gm.md","lastUpdated":null}'),n={name:"logistics/src/page-6aq5YsEznFHSQK7mwkc1Gm.md"};function o(s,e,l,c,p,g){return i(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[t('<div class="logistics-content"><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="_2-4-阻塞信号の-超光速-反向传播规律" aria-hidden="true"></span><h1 id="_2-4-faster-than-light-upstream-propagation-of-blocking-signals" tabindex="-1">2.4 “Faster-Than-Light” Upstream Propagation of Blocking Signals <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-4-faster-than-light-upstream-propagation-of-blocking-signals" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2\\.4 “Faster-Than-Light” Upstream Propagation of Blocking Signals&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Source page ID: <code>6aq5YsEznFHSQK7mwkc1Gm</code> ｜ <a href="https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=6aq5YsEznFHSQK7mwkc1Gm" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open in Tencent Docs</a></p></blockquote><details><summary>The AI summary returns</summary><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-6d15f24d857bdba7.q90-d8ba377b7428cc57.webp" alt="24 “Faster-Than-Light” Upstream Propagation of Blocking Signals"></p></details><p>We often speak of <strong>modules</strong>. Here a module is a <strong>production network</strong> performing a particular task. Recall that production networks have input and output nodes; a module&#39;s are naturally the <u>inputs and finished products</u> we want.</p><p>Consider a classic example, ignoring other material consumption: a <span class="mark-blue">Yazhen Syringe [C] module uses 20 Ferrium Ore for 1 Yazhen Syringe [C]</span>, while an <span class="mark-orange">SC battery module uses 5 Ferrium Ore and 20 Dense Originium Powder for 1 SC Wuling Battery</span>. Input production is 120 Ferrium Ore and 295 Dense Originium Powder per minute.</p><p>Every Endministrator knows to prioritize batteries: they have higher value and a limited possible quantity. Blue medicine requires only Ferrium Ore as a hard constraint and consumes whatever Ferrium Ore is available, providing a fallback like simply selling Xiranite. Calculation gives <u>14.75 SC batteries/min, consuming 73.75 Ferrium Ore, with all remaining 46.25 Ferrium Ore going to medicine</u>.</p><p>This ratio is awkward to obtain with fixed-ratio splitting, so consider <strong>blocking-based production</strong>. Using priority, or splitting in the next section, <strong>block the battery module&#39;s Ferrium Ore</strong> to reach its planned rate. How? It consumes at most 73.75 Ferrium Ore/min before exhausting powder; <u>without powder, Ferrium Ore naturally blocks</u>. Its consumption settles at the corresponding rate.</p><p>Once blocking forms, simply <u>give the remaining Ferrium Ore a route to medicine</u> by connecting the medicine module. It automatically produces medicine corresponding to 46.25 Ferrium Ore—<em>because that is all the Ferrium Ore we have</em>.</p><p>For Ferrium Ore, these modules clearly exhibit <strong>controlled/free branch</strong> roles. <strong>The battery module is controlled</strong>: high-priority Ferrium Ore blocks there. <span class="mark-red">Its Ferrium Ore consumption is controlled by Originium Powder supply.</span> <strong>The medicine module is Ferrium Ore&#39;s free branch</strong>: lower-priority Ferrium Ore does not block there. Its capacity is generally designed to accept all remaining Ferrium Ore, and <span class="mark-red">its actual rate depends on incoming Ferrium Ore flow</span>, overflowing from the controlled branch.</p><p>How do we build the line so <strong>Ferrium Ore blocking in the battery module propagates correctly back to Ferrium Ore supply</strong>? Trace the <strong>blocking chain</strong>. Basic blocking-flow behavior gives an obvious rule:</p><p>Material moves downstream, but <strong>blocking signals propagate upstream</strong>. When belts are full, they can propagate instantly, without waiting for buffer filling. This is <strong>upstream propagation of blocking signals</strong>. Events may be simultaneous, but causally the first location to block is the source of blocking, whose signal blocks its immediate upstream.</p><blockquote><p>A blockage in one location often immediately changes another part of a line. This “spooky action at a distance” exemplifies instantaneous blocking-signal propagation.</p></blockquote><p>Trace the chain in the diagram below. The source of Ferrium Ore blocking is clearly the Packaging Unit, manifested as Xircon blocking. Since the Reactor Crucible makes Xircon from Xircon Effluent and Ferrium Powder, both inputs block. Propagation continues upstream through Ferrium Powder and Ferrium, finally overflowing Ferrium Ore into the left-hand free branch.</p><blockquote><p>Incidentally, the Xircon Effluent blocking signal also propagates upstream; that is not our focus here.</p></blockquote><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-cb1db89ae37ce34d.q90-08313d759e1c1293.webp" alt="24 “Faster-Than-Light” Upstream Propagation of Blocking Signals"></p><p>Tracing blocking chains is a basic design skill. <strong>Only correct analysis ensures a blockage triggers the intended change.</strong> If this example feels too simple, analyze the 73.75-Xircon design with three Expanded Crucibles and one Reactor Crucible, supplied externally with 12 Xircon Effluent, 18.4 Liquid Xiranite, and sufficient Clean Water and Sewage. Devise a layout where Xircon blocking due to missing Originium Powder propagates correctly to the external 18.4 Liquid Xiranite.</p><p><a href="./page-t2SxyX0ruN8BvrYjwgoBde">2.3 The Controlled Controlled Branch and the Unfree Free Branch (Considering Retirement)</a></p><p><a href="./page-o3yxqfGNALKamNYPO1eu1t">2.5 Is a Stash Not Just a Special Splitter? (Core Content ⭐)</a></p></div>',1)])])}const d=r(n,[["render",o]]);export{m as __pageData,d as default};
